<h1>Orcraft Test Group</h1>
<p>A group collects related tests. Unlike a test, a group has no body: it is declared, not coded. Its
  config names the tests it holds, and the framework does the rest.</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from __future__ import annotations

from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTestGroup,
	OrcraftTestGroupConfig,
	OrcraftTestGroupResult,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)

from my_group.my_first_test.my_first_test import MyFirstTest
from my_group.my_second_test.my_second_test import MySecondTest


class MyGroupConfig(OrcraftTestGroupConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Group")
	description = OrcraftConfigField.Text(
		default="Groups the tests that cover one area of the system under test."
	)
	tests = OrcraftConfigField.Tests(
		default_factory=lambda: [
			MyFirstTest(),
			MySecondTest(),
		]
	)


class MyGroupResult(OrcraftTestGroupResult):
	pass


class MyGroup(OrcraftTestGroup):
	def __init__(self) -> None:
		super().__init__(config=MyGroupConfig())
		self.result = MyGroupResult()


def main() -> None:
	MyGroup().run()


if __name__ == "__main__":
	main()') }}

<h2 id="tests-field">Tests field</h2>
<p><code>tests</code> uses <code>default_factory</code> rather than <code>default</code> because it
  constructs objects. A factory is called once per config instance, so two group instances never end up
  sharing the same test objects - a mistake that a plain <code>default</code> list would make easy and
  hard to spot.</p>

<p>The result class is usually empty. Subclassing <code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code> without adding
  anything is still worth doing: it gives the group a result type of its own to extend later, and keeps
  the three-class shape consistent across the suite.</p>

<h2 id="why-group">Purpose</h2>
<p>A group is the unit the controller lets you act on as a whole:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Configure once, apply to many.</strong> Setting a parameter at group level applies it to
    every test in the group, so a shared executable folder or endpoint is entered once rather than per
    test.</li>
  <li><strong>Run a slice.</strong> A group can be run without the rest of the suite, which is how a
    focused rerun stays focused.</li>
  <li><strong>Aggregate reporting.</strong> Group results carry both test counters and case counters, so
    a group answers how many of its tests came back clean and how many cases that took.</li>
</ul>

<p>The tutorial works through the first two in
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/running-a-group/">Running a group</a>.</p>

<h2 id="config-fields">Config fields</h2>
<h3 id="config-code">Overridable in code</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, fixed for the run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>name</code></td>
      <td><code>"Test Group"</code></td>
      <td>Display name in the GUI, CLI and logs. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>description</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Free text describing what the group does. Not editable from the GUI.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>version</code></td>
      <td><code>""</code></td>
      <td>Group version, recorded with the results.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>tests</code></td>
      <td><code>[]</code></td>
      <td>The tests this group runs.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>parallel</code></td>
      <td><code>True</code></td>
      <td>Whether the group's tests may run concurrently.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_orientation</code></td>
      <td><code>HORIZONTAL</code></td>
      <td>Orientation of the logged config table.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Redeclare a field with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default -
    <code>name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Group")</code>. A bare assignment such as
    <code>name = "My Group"</code> is not an override: it shadows the field with a plain attribute, and the
    GUI, the log table and <code>required</code> validation no longer see it.</p>
</div>

<h3 id="config-gui">Overridable in code or GUI</h3>
<p>Set in your config class, and changeable again from the run configuration panel before a run.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Default</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>upload_test_package</code></td>
      <td><code>"if_missing"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the test package is uploaded to the endpoint before the run.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>update_endpoint_app</code></td>
      <td><code>"defer"</code></td>
      <td>Whether the endpoint application is updated before the run. <code>defer</code> leaves the decision to the level below.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--warning">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Override carefully</span>
  <p>Same rule as above: redeclare with the same <code>OrcraftConfigField</code> type and a new default,
    never a bare value. The default you set here is what the run configuration panel shows until someone
    changes it there.</p>
</div>


<h3 id="config-reserved">Reserved</h3>
<p>Written by the framework while a run is in progress.</p>

<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Field</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>start_time</code></td>
      <td>When the run started. Stamped by the framework at run time - leave it alone.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_tab</code></td>
      <td>Log indentation. Assigned by the framework at every level - it cannot be overridden, only collided with.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>log_title_color</code>, <code>log_title_bg_color</code></td>
      <td>Palette plumbing for the log banners. Not user-facing.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<div class="docs-callout docs-callout--caution">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Do not redeclare</span>
  <p>A field of your own under one of these names replaces the framework's. The framework keeps writing
    to the name, but through your definition - and the log indentation, the run timestamps and the
    endpoint a step is dispatched to silently stop meaning what the controller expects.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="group-result-fields">Result fields</h2>
<p>A group has no body, so its result is written entirely by the framework as its tests complete. An
  empty <code>OrcraftTestGroupResult</code> subclass therefore reports everything a group needs;
  declaring fields of your own is for the rare group that computes something across its tests.</p>

<p class="docs-legend"><span class="mark mark--user">{% icon "check" %}</span> overridable by user (optional)
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--framework">{% icon "cpu" %}</span> written by the framework, may still be set directly
  &nbsp;&middot;&nbsp; <span class="mark mark--reserved">{% icon "shield-lock" %}</span> reserved</p>

